3. Debug a failing service (the real exercise)

Create a service that fails on purpose:

bash
sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/broken.service > /dev/null <<'EOF'
[Unit]
Description=Deliberately broken demo

[Service]
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/does-not-exist
EOF

sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl start broken.service

It will fail. Now investigate like you would on the job:

bash
systemctl status broken.service
journalctl -u broken.service --no-pager
systemctl show broken.service -p Result -p ExecMainStatus -p ExecMainCode

You’ll see status=203/EXEC, which means systemd couldn’t execute the binary. Learning to read these codes is a big part of the skill. Common ones:

203/EXEC: binary missing or not executable
200/CHDIR, 217/USER: bad working directory or user
1 (or other small numbers): the program itself ran and exited with an error
status=SEGV / ABRT: the program crashed

Now make it fail differently. Edit the file (sudo systemctl edit --full broken.service) and change ExecStart to:

ini
ExecStart=/bin/sh -c 'echo "starting up"; echo "config missing" >&2; exit 1'

Then:

bash
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl start broken.service
journalctl -u broken.service --no-pager

This time the log shows both lines, because journald captures stdout and stderr. The failure is now in the app, not systemd.

Next, add crash looping and watch it:

ini
[Service]
ExecStart=/bin/sh -c 'echo "crashing"; exit 1'
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=2
bash
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl start broken.service
journalctl -u broken.service -f

You’ll see it restart repeatedly until systemd hits its start limit and gives up (Start request repeated too quickly). To clear that state:

bash
sudo systemctl reset-failed broken.service

Cleanup:

bash
sudo systemctl stop broken.service
sudo rm -f /etc/systemd/system/broken.service
sudo rm -rf /etc/systemd/system/broken.service.d
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl reset-failed